WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
(calm music)

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.553
- What's up, guys?

00:00:05.553 --> 00:00:08.610
Michael here once again at
Wisecrack's super secret

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.730
offsite location to talk
about the biggest lesson

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.070
millennials have learned since
the 2008 real estate crisis:

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:16.730
that there's nothing more fun

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.330
than watching people buy houses on TV.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.590
Whether it's identical
twin-led home makeovers,

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.980
identical twins who have
definitely kissed by the way,

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.240
quirky cabin renovators, or
a fixer-upper power couple

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.900
slowly building an empire,

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.930
the fact of the matter is folks under 40

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:35.380
are the primary consumers
of channels like HGTV

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:36.640
and the Magnolia Network,

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.540
and it isn't just about television.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.820
Younger adults are gawking at dream homes

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.100
and turnkey disasters on
Instagram, TikTok, Pinterest,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.600
and of course on websites
like StreetEasy, realtor.com,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.290
Trulia, and perhaps the most
infamous of them all, Zillow.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.810
- I'd never live in North
Carolina, but if I did,

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.819
I could buy a big, gross mansion.

00:00:58.819 --> 00:00:59.840
(audience laughing)

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:00.800
- Zillow.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.480
- But why are we so glued to
these visions of property?

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.850
Is it all escapist fantasy for homes

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.330
we'll never be able to afford?

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.430
Are these forays actually preparing us

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.280
for the realities of the
American real estate landscape?

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.440
Or is our obsession with real estate shows

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.080
and online listings
inadvertently helping us

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.270
to dig our own property grave?

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.547
Let's find out in this "Wisecrack
Edition: Home Ownership,

00:01:22.547 --> 00:01:23.940
"What Went Wrong?"

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.980
Americans have coveted property
since the first Europeans

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.440
laid claim to this already inhabited land.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.380
In fact, taking a nod from
philosopher John Locke,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.630
the Declaration of Independence
touted every human being's

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.277
inalienable right to, "Life, liberty,

00:01:38.277 --> 00:01:39.840
"and the pursuit of happiness."

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:40.930
But here's the catch.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.640
Locke was better known for his thoughts

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.620
on the right to property

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:48.020
and for his complicated views
on the pursuit of happiness.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.130
Thus, Thomas Jefferson's phrase of choice

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.990
has often been interpreted
to mean property.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.440
And we can only assume
that the Founding Fathers

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.910
would've been huge HGTV fans,

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.650
because they like to watch brothers kiss.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:03.160
The good news was there was
plenty of land in North America

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.790
for disaffected Europeans to plant flags

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.170
and finally call dibs
on their own property.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:11.400
This was in stark contrast
to the situation back home.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.597
As historian Gordon S. Wood wrote,

00:02:13.597 --> 00:02:15.737
"Land was scarce in relation to people

00:02:15.737 --> 00:02:17.397
"and therefore was expensive.

00:02:17.397 --> 00:02:19.927
"Hence, unable to afford
their own land to farm,

00:02:19.927 --> 00:02:22.397
"Europeans were compelled
to work for others,

00:02:22.397 --> 00:02:24.487
"either by becoming
laborers for landowners

00:02:24.487 --> 00:02:25.387
"in the countryside,

00:02:25.387 --> 00:02:27.927
"or more often by migrating to the cities

00:02:27.927 --> 00:02:30.910
"to engage in manufacturing
goods in factories."

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.570
Because it was easy to find people

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:36.020
to fill both kinds of jobs, wages sucked.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:38.360
As a result, a lot of underpaid

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.500
non-property-owning European workers

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.050
put off marriage and kids.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:43.680
Wow, putting off marriage and kids

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.370
because you can't make enough money?

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.539
That would never happen to anyone I know.

00:02:47.539 --> 00:02:48.860
(melancholy music)

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.710
I wish I had kids that
would love me, but I don't.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:52.810
And even though the Founding
Fathers may have hated

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.760
the oppressive system they fled,
they somewhat recreated it.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.570
It's like how you worked your whole life

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.100
not to become your parents,

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:01.350
but you look in the mirror one day

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.240
and you realize you're just like them.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.420
Under capitalism, property
ownership does equal liberty,

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.480
at least to some degree.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.810
- We wanna live where
everyone else vacations.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.100
- Home ownership in
America is the best way

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.780
to build generational wealth.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.610
So if your family historically
did not have access

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.740
to property for reasons,

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.600
you have a much smaller
chance of upward mobility

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.670
than people whose families
have been able to use homes

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.400
as assets, equity, and inheritances.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.400
Having trouble paying down a debt?

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.610
If you're a homeowner, you could refinance

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.370
or take out a second mortgage.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:37.240
If you're not, you can, I don't know,

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:40.240
like sell bodily fluids.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:41.800
According to the federal reserve,

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.590
the median net worth for
homeowners in 2019 was $255,000

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.810
compared to a paltry $6,300 for renters.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.880
If you're a homeowner, it's also likely

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.070
that you around a lot of other homeowners.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.680
That means you and your neighbors
are paying property taxes

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.570
into the local schools that
result in better funding

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:03.380
than neighborhoods with lower income

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.090
and higher proportions of renters.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:07.340
So your kids not only likely benefit

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:10.960
from generational wealth,
but from a better education.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.200
And what a cool system

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.420
where the quality of a child's education

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.583
is tied to property tax
rates in their neighborhood.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:20.470
I see no problems with this.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.640
And let's not forget that
property ownership was used

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:25.460
for deliberately exclusionary purposes,

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.360
like restricting voting rights
to minorities and women.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.830
In fact, even white men
who didn't own property

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.360
were often disenfranchised
from the voting process

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.930
until the mid 19th century.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:38.200
As Katherine Levine Einstein
and Maxwell Palmer point out,

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.987
even today, "Homeowners dominate

00:04:39.987 --> 00:04:41.647
"contemporary local politics.

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:43.667
"They participate at far higher rates,

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:45.237
"are electorally powerful,

00:04:45.237 --> 00:04:47.797
"and comprise virtually
all elected officials,

00:04:47.797 --> 00:04:50.287
"even in cities with large
majorities of renters.

00:04:50.287 --> 00:04:51.927
"Their disproportionate influence

00:04:51.927 --> 00:04:53.427
"comes with stark consequences

00:04:53.427 --> 00:04:55.187
"for American local governments.

00:04:55.187 --> 00:04:56.727
"Land use and housing policies

00:04:56.727 --> 00:04:58.117
"that favor the interest of homeowners

00:04:58.117 --> 00:05:00.127
"have spurred high housing costs,

00:05:00.127 --> 00:05:03.147
"sprawling and environmentally
destructive land use,

00:05:03.147 --> 00:05:05.087
"racial and economic segregation,

00:05:05.087 --> 00:05:08.670
"and unequal access to
high quality public goods."

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.600
At the Constitutional Convention,

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.870
the question of whether
you needed to own property

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.210
to participate politically
was hotly contested,

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.950
and folks like future
president James Madison argued

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.030
only land owners could be trusted

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.690
with our delicate Republic.

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:24.810
See, in Madison's mind, realistically,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.080
most Americans would never own property.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.840
And if all those unpropertied
people banded together

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:31.820
and, say, voted in unison,

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:35.500
well that would be bad news
bears for the upper crust.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.440
But like, what if we did
that for real though.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:38.610
Others at the Convention,

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.350
like kite enthusiast
Benjamin Franklin, disagreed,

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:42.750
concerned that land ownership

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:45.200
as a condition of electoral participation

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.380
would create a powerful aristocracy

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.130
and powerless lower class.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.640
Besides, many in the latter
group, he pointed out,

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.540
had fought in the recent Revolution

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:57.120
that allowed these guys to even
be having this conversation.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.360
Ultimately, the Constitution ended up

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.160
with little to say about property,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:03.450
and decisions about whether
you needed to own property

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.220
to vote were left to individual states

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.650
who got cruelly creative.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.640
As we moved forward, and
especially in the Jim Crow era,

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:13.040
local restrictions on voting

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.150
and political participation increased.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.960
At the same time,
discriminatory housing policies

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:21.190
made it incredibly difficult
for pretty much anyone

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:24.580
who wasn't a white male
to own anything at all.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.430
The emphasis on property ownership

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:30.410
as the paragon of American
virtue was intentionally designed

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.420
to benefit one class of people

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.340
to the exclusion of all others.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:36.890
So in some ways, owning
your little piece of land

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:39.800
has always been crucial
to success in America.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.900
At the same time, it's
remained an elusive dream

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.030
for so many.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.710
And today that includes older millennials.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:49.990
They remain deeply impacted
by the 2008 financial crisis

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:52.070
that saw millions of Americans lose homes

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:54.310
to predatory subprime lending practices.

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:57.940
And they saw good paying jobs
disappear almost overnight.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:06:59.460
And, of course, they're further saddled

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.090
with an unprecedented
amount of student loan debt,

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:05.150
which really counts into your
white picket fence budget.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:08.310
Or your food budget, rent
budget, toothpaste budget.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.880
Just your budget as a whole
'cause you don't have any money.

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.630
And that's not great
because studies have shown

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.220
that renting is literally
bad for your health.

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:19.270
A 2012 study of urban
homeowners and renters reported

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:22.087
that homeowners perceived,
"A sense of control,

00:07:22.087 --> 00:07:24.390
"trust in neighbors, and
residential stability"

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:26.530
that resulted in better
mental health outcomes

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.140
than their renting counterparts.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:30.080
Unsurprisingly, the lack of stability

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.220
that has defined millennial adulthood

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.090
leads to depression, anxiety,

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:37.430
and a fertile landscape of
Instagram and TikTok therapy.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:40.730
But then (sighs) the pandemic hit.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.830
And while cooped up in tiny apartments,

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:44.700
we started gazing longingly

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:47.000
at the internet listings
of homes with yards,

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.660
room for an office, and enough
space to escape the scent

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.840
of our roommate's
deteriorating hygiene habits.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.750
- And you know how I
am about my gas range.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:56.640
- [Michael] But as young
people flocked to the internet

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:57.637
to ogle their dream homes,

00:07:57.637 --> 00:08:00.990
one of the main purveyors
of #housegoalscontent

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:02.880
found itself in hot water

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.420
for deeply anti-consumer practices.

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:08.020
And it managed to also
royally screw itself

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.870
in an ill-conceived effort
to profit even further

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.410
off the home selling process.

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:14.900
That website is Zillow.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.880
And if you're between
the ages of 25 and 40,

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.850
you've probably lost at
least one afternoon to it.

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.359
- Are you bored?

00:08:22.359 --> 00:08:23.550
- Mm-hmm.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.250
- [Michael] Launched in 2006,

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:27.680
Zillow began as a simple listings website

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:30.420
where users could put in specs
for homes they wanted to buy,

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:32.290
things like location, budget,

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:34.190
numbers of bedrooms and
bathrooms, et cetera.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:35.023
- I go to open houses

00:08:35.023 --> 00:08:36.660
'cause I love seeing
how other people live.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:38.650
- Then they could connect with a broker

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:40.320
when they saw something they liked.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.497
According to Will Parker
of the Wall Street Journal,

00:08:42.497 --> 00:08:45.047
"It's a platform for real
estate agents to find people

00:08:45.047 --> 00:08:47.117
"who are looking for homes
and want to buy them.

00:08:47.117 --> 00:08:49.597
"That's the primary way
that Zillow makes money,

00:08:49.597 --> 00:08:50.647
"and it's a good business.

00:08:50.647 --> 00:08:52.397
"It's very high margins.

00:08:52.397 --> 00:08:54.557
"And so it's not a very capital

00:08:54.557 --> 00:08:56.600
"or asset intensive business."

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:57.880
Side note, if you wanna learn more

00:08:57.880 --> 00:08:59.520
about how platforms function,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.337
check out our recent video on Elon Musk.

00:09:02.337 --> 00:09:05.910
But in 2018, the company
decided to cut out the middleman

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:07.920
and start buying and
selling homes directly,

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:11.690
a practice called instant
buying or iBuying,

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.340
because when you put
I in front of anything

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:14.550
it makes it fancy.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.350
And they predicted it would
make them, in real estate terms,

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:19.540
a metric butt full of money.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:21.317
Journalists Ryan Knutson put it this way,

00:09:21.317 --> 00:09:24.327
"With iBuying, consumers
could skip the long hassle

00:09:24.327 --> 00:09:27.187
"of listing a home, showing
it, waiting for offers,

00:09:27.187 --> 00:09:28.667
"negotiating, and so on,

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:31.057
"and instead sell their
home almost instantly

00:09:31.057 --> 00:09:32.307
"to a company like Zillow.

00:09:32.307 --> 00:09:34.447
"Zillow thought this model
would be more attractive

00:09:34.447 --> 00:09:36.050
"to buyers and sellers."

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:39.390
And as Parker points out, even
if the price Zillow offered

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:41.720
for your house was lower
than its market value,

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.630
the convenience of the transaction

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:46.090
and the promise that the
deal would go through quickly

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:48.560
was attractive enough
to lure in more sellers

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:50.310
and grow Zillow's inventory.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:53.010
In essence, Zillow was
simply home flipping,

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:56.160
a practice any HGTV
viewer is familiar with.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.670
- The asking price was 390,000,

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:01.028
and we wound up paying 9
billion, plus closing costs.

00:10:01.028 --> 00:10:01.861
(audience laughing)

00:10:01.861 --> 00:10:03.900
- Ideally, Zillow would buy homes

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:06.430
and then sell them for more
than they purchased them.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:08.520
On top of that, iBuyers bring in money

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:12.380
through transaction fees, the
real cash cow in the deal.

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.810
Zillow predicted that
their iBuying scheme,

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:16.760
which they called Zillow Offers,

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.970
would rake in an extra 20 billion a year.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:23.360
But house flipping isn't that
lucrative on a small scale.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:26.100
You can't, say, buy a house for 100,000

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.240
and sell it for a million.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.850
The margin between buying
costs and selling profits

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.430
is relatively small.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:33.780
So in order to live up
to the massive promise

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:35.920
of a multi-billion
dollar surge in revenue,

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.330
Zillow had to go big or go home.

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:41.040
Or go big and get homes.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.410
And that's exactly what they did.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.450
Zillow snapped up 7,000 homes

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:47.600
at prices that baffled their competitors,

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:49.710
and in many cases delighted homeowners

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.260
who were putting their
houses on the market.

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:53.530
Zillow would sometimes
pay more than the price

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:56.070
at which local real estate
agents had valued the homes

00:10:56.070 --> 00:10:57.360
in order to entice the seller

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:59.100
to make an easy and quick decision.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.040
And this practice inspired
a now viral TikTok

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:04.690
where real estate agents
Sean Gotcher posits-

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:09.210
- Why would they pay
340 for this 31st home

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:12.630
whenever they've only
paid 300 for these others?

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:16.450
Well, what that just did
is create a new comp.

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:19.550
So when they go to sell
these other 30 homes,

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:21.100
that extra $40,000 that you could say

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:25.130
this ones sold for 340
just made them 1.2 million.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.630
- Of course, Zillow tried to
shoot down these insinuations

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.787
in a statement to Yahoo
News, claiming that,

00:11:29.787 --> 00:11:33.037
"We pay market value for
every home we purchase.

00:11:33.037 --> 00:11:34.887
"When we looked at homes
that sold traditionally

00:11:34.887 --> 00:11:36.737
"after they declined a Zillow offer,

00:11:36.737 --> 00:11:38.157
"we learned that on average,

00:11:38.157 --> 00:11:42.177
"those selling traditionally
sold for only 0.09% more

00:11:42.177 --> 00:11:43.260
"than the Zillow offer."

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.720
And if we learned anything
about corporate PR,

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:48.000
we can always trust exactly what they say.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.530
But quibbles over price fixing aside,

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:52.730
Zillow's plan had a few kinks.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:55.000
Real estate isn't a Costco,

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.910
and snapping up houses in
bulk is considerably riskier

00:11:57.910 --> 00:11:59.980
than impulse buying those five-ton cartons

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:01.320
of pizza-flavored Goldfish.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:02.930
Although, realistically,

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:04.900
both are gonna leave
you with some regrets.

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:07.800
And in the fall of 2021, regret hit hard

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:09.920
when Zillow announced that it had taken

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:14.100
a half billion dollar
loss on Zillow Offers,

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:18.550
which, if you're counting,
is $20.5 billion less

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:20.070
than the profit they'd expected.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:22.590
It's hard not to collapse
at Zillow's hubris,

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:25.660
but it's instructive to consider
what they're planning to do

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:26.810
with all those houses.

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:28.310
And spoiler alert,

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:30.730
they're not selling them
to families or newlyweds.

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:32.391
According to Bloomberg News,

00:12:32.391 --> 00:12:34.890
Zillow's pitching them to
institutional investors

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:36.160
like private equity firms.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.930
And this whole debacle
is really a microcosm

00:12:38.930 --> 00:12:40.950
of a much larger problem in America.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.690
In the aftermath of the
2008 housing crisis,

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:46.530
huge corporate investors
started scooping up houses

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:48.810
that had been dispossessed
and renting them out,

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:51.870
often for more than residents
would've paid otherwise.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.690
This practice has ramped
up aggressively since

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.240
with corporations like Blackstone

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.960
pouring billions of
dollars into real estate

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:00.670
and yielding record profits.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:02.480
For example, in Atlanta last year,

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:05.170
17% of single family homes were bought

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:08.680
by large corporate investors
according to Atlanta Studies.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:11.630
See, big corporations are
often willing to take big risks

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.450
and big losses on properties,

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:16.860
something the average individual
or family can't afford.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.660
At the same time, by
artificially constraining

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.450
the supply of real estate in America,

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:24.590
these corporations have
driven prices sky high.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:27.370
As such, neighborhood dynamics are upended

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:29.710
as families and businesses are priced out

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:30.800
of their communities.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.440
And we're not alone in this.

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:34.900
America's maple syrup-soaked
neighbors to the north

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:36.680
are experiencing something similar.

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:40.180
Canadian publication The
Walrus illustrated the problem

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:42.430
with the story of S.W. Welch,

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:44.530
an independent used bookstore in Montreal.

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:46.040
When the building housing the bookstore

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:47.830
was bought by a real estate firm,

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:49.860
68-year-old Stephen Welch was informed

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:51.790
that the store's rent, taxes, insurance,

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:54.660
and maintenance would
increase to 5,000 a month,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:57.600
a staggering 150%.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:59.777
Unable to afford the
surge, Welch said simply,

00:13:59.777 --> 00:14:01.627
"I think they'd rather
the building be empty

00:14:01.627 --> 00:14:02.760
"than have me here."

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:04.070
And increasingly this is true

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:06.080
throughout the United States as well.

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.840
For a real estate firm that
can afford to buy up properties

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:10.910
and lucrative locations
and leave them empty,

00:14:10.910 --> 00:14:12.160
it's a waiting game.

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:14.460
As executive director of Downtown Montreal

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:16.997
Business Association Glen
Castanheira explained,

00:14:16.997 --> 00:14:19.577
"Say a landlord buys a property
with a mom and pop shop

00:14:19.577 --> 00:14:22.317
"that pays its rent on time every month,

00:14:22.317 --> 00:14:24.107
"but it only pays $1,000.

00:14:24.107 --> 00:14:26.597
"The landlord may think he can get 5,000

00:14:26.597 --> 00:14:27.857
"or 10,000 per month.

00:14:27.857 --> 00:14:30.537
"Even if the property
remains vacant for 10 months,

00:14:30.537 --> 00:14:33.397
"it only takes one month's
rent with the new tenant

00:14:33.397 --> 00:14:35.500
"to make back what they lost."

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:38.700
Some cities have created taxes
and fees to penalize firms

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:40.560
who leave usable property vacant

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.020
for extended periods of time.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:45.350
But real estate speculation,
the act of buying up property

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:48.060
in anticipation of favorable
changes in the market,

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.650
has proven too profitable to be shut down,

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:52.530
especially by slaps on the wrist

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:55.850
like Oakland, California's
6,000 a year flat fee

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:58.160
for homes in use fewer
than 50 days a year.

00:14:58.160 --> 00:14:59.930
And in a red hot housing market,

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:02.840
some 16 million homes still remain vacant

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:04.460
throughout the United States.

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:07.940
The injustice of there being
16 million vacant homes

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:10.930
in a country with half a
million unhoused people

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:12.640
is pretty staggering.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.340
Meanwhile, since the recession,

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:16.900
the value of American
real estate has ballooned

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:18.530
at an unprecedented rate.

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:21.000
But remember, lots of
homeowners lost their houses

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:21.910
during the crisis.

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:24.380
So instead of these
gains going to moderate

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:25.970
or middle income homeowners,

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:28.720
it's going into the coffers
of huge corporations

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:29.930
and their shareholders.

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:32.800
And it's all fun, games,
and easy television

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.290
for our glowing surrogates

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:36.950
in their newly remodeled
happily ever afters.

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:41.290
But when you turn the TV off,
the reality is a lot darker.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:43.310
One survey referenced in Fortune

00:15:43.310 --> 00:15:45.650
showed that some 82% of millennials

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:48.830
have at least one significant
regret about buying a home,

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:50.710
including decreasing home values,

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.130
neighborhoods that turn
out to be a bad fit,

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:56.220
and the decidedly unglamorous
realities of fixer-uppers.

00:15:56.220 --> 00:16:00.330
Meanwhile, foreclosures
spiked a staggering 700%

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:04.270
in January 2022, according
to data company Black Knight.

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:06.120
This picture doesn't quite square

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:08.700
with the cheer antics of
"Christina on the Coast."

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:10.770
So what about the majority of us

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:14.140
left window shopping on Zillow,
fantasizing about the day

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:16.470
we too will join the ranks of landowners?

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:18.520
In some ways we're cosplaying a dream

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:20.710
in stark contrast to reality.

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:23.120
Put differently, we're situating ourselves

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.020
squarely within a system

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:26.910
that's rendered us pretty powerless.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:30.267
Iris Marion Young contends
that, "The goal of a dream house

00:16:30.267 --> 00:16:32.577
"sets workers working and
keeps workers working,

00:16:32.577 --> 00:16:34.657
"fearing job loss, working overtime.

00:16:34.657 --> 00:16:37.487
"The consumer-driven
desire of civic privatism

00:16:37.487 --> 00:16:39.950
"tends to produce political quietism."

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.160
That is to say, when we're really worried

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:44.280
about finding our own single family house,

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:45.600
we're gonna work our butts off

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.880
instead of questioning
the political system

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:49.570
that puts us in this position.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:52.690
As sociologist David Madden
and urban planner Peter Marcuse

00:16:52.690 --> 00:16:54.617
write in their book "In
Defense of Housing,"

00:16:54.617 --> 00:16:56.927
"Ownership restricts household
members' opportunities

00:16:56.927 --> 00:16:58.347
"to engage in other activities,

00:16:58.347 --> 00:17:00.497
"as well as their desires to do so,

00:17:00.497 --> 00:17:02.407
"especially their
willingness to participate

00:17:02.407 --> 00:17:05.890
"in collective action that
might involve social conflict."

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:08.810
When we're more focused on
benefiting from a (beep) system,

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:11.730
we're less likely to
agitate against that system.

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.730
At the same time, the writers argue

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:16.097
that this individualist
thinking means that,

00:17:16.097 --> 00:17:17.387
"If something goes wrong,

00:17:17.387 --> 00:17:19.967
"the individual is thought to be to blame

00:17:19.967 --> 00:17:22.630
"rather than social and
political structures."

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:25.350
The fact that so many
millennials can't buy a house

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:27.630
or can't keep up with
their mortgage payments

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:28.960
isn't because they're blowing

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:30.800
their not-keeping-up-with-inflation wages

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:32.300
on avocado toast.

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:35.500
It's because they're being
priced out by huge corporations.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:38.300
Zillow's accessibility belies that reality

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:40.340
and keeps us dreaming,

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:42.900
and that's what makes it so seductive.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.480
But what do you guys think?

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:47.000
Are you still gonna spend your
Friday nights on a hot date

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.810
with the Zillow listings and a
rerun of "Property Brothers"?

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:53.350
Or is real estate porn
actually a lot more sinister

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:55.400
than the other twins that do real estate

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:56.670
might have you think?

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:58.770
Let us know in the comments.

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:00.110
Huge things to all our patrons.

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:01.770
And if you haven't
checked it out in a while,

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:02.940
check out our Patreon page.

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:04.950
We have a lot of cool new stuff there.

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:07.650
Also, check out our new
stream Wisecrack Live

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:09.750
every Thursday right here.

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:10.990
Hit that subscribe button

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:13.470
like you're clicking Buy
on the Zillow listing

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.950
for the French Riviera
castle of your dreams,

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:17.870
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:20.466
And as always, thanks for watching. Later.

00:18:20.466 --> 00:18:23.133
(upbeat music)

